["<aside class=\"callout why\" role=\"note\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Coast deer do not spread out evenly. Food, cover and snow pull them to a few places each season. Know the places and you stop walking empty forest.</p></aside><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 330\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Where black tailed deer are through the year. Spring: logged openings with new green food. Summer: some move up, even to subalpine. Fall, your hunt: the rut; snow up high pushes them down. Winter: old growth and steep south to west slopes. Snow deeper than 30 cm is hard going.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"330\" fill=\"#EEF1EA\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Where they are through the year</text>\n<g data-tip=\"Spring|Logged openings: new green food\"><rect x=\"14\" y=\"48\" width=\"372\" height=\"52\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"28\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Spring</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"91\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Logged openings: new green food</text></g><g data-tip=\"Summer|Some move up, even to subalpine\"><rect x=\"14\" y=\"110\" width=\"372\" height=\"52\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"28\" y=\"132\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Summer</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"153\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Some move up, even to subalpine</text></g><g data-tip=\"Fall: your hunt|Rut. Snow up high pushes them down (Tip)\"><rect x=\"14\" y=\"172\" width=\"372\" height=\"52\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#FF6A13\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"28\" y=\"194\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#FF6A13\">Fall: your hunt</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"215\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Rut. Snow up high pushes them down (Tip)</text></g><g data-tip=\"Winter|Old growth; steep south to west slopes\"><rect x=\"14\" y=\"234\" width=\"372\" height=\"52\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"28\" y=\"256\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Winter</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"277\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Old growth; steep south to west slopes</text></g><text x=\"200\" y=\"316\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Snow deeper than 30 cm is hard going (90%)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Spring and summer: logged openings, new green food <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Winter: old growth and steep south to west slopes <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the year in detail</summary><ul><li>\"Black-tailed deer ... make frequent use of logged habitats during spring and summer\" (90%, BC deer account 2000).</li><li>In summer most deer move higher for new growth; some stay low all year <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>. Some coast deer go as high as subalpine and alpine in summer (90%, Nyberg and Janz 1990).</li><li>Winter: old growth is a key part of coast winter range and \"critical for their survival\": it shelters, holds snow off the ground and drops branches and lichens to eat (90%, BC deer account).</li><li>Farther north and higher up, steep south to west facing sites make the best winter range <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Snow deeper than 30 cm is hard going for deer <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>November near Harrison: rut, plus the first snow up high. Snow up high is likely to push deer down toward winter range (Tip). The usual first snow date at Harrison elevations: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>Earlier chapter: <a href=\"#/s/mc-black-tailed-deer-01\">Meet the deer</a>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 318\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Two kinds of deer. Resident: about 140 hectares a year in one area. Migrant: separate summer and winter ranges, often 2 to 5 km apart. In the moderate snow zone 20 to 25% of deer migrate; in the deep snow zone 70% or more.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"318\" fill=\"#EEF1EA\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Stay home or move?</text>\n<rect x=\"14\" y=\"46\" width=\"180\" height=\"210\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"206\" y=\"46\" width=\"180\" height=\"210\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Resident</text><text x=\"296\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Migrant</text><circle cx=\"104\" cy=\"150\" r=\"46\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"146\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#ffffff\">about</text><text x=\"104\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">140 ha</text><circle cx=\"296\" cy=\"112\" r=\"30\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"296\" y=\"117\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#ffffff\">summer</text><circle cx=\"296\" cy=\"214\" r=\"30\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"296\" y=\"219\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#ffffff\">winter</text><path d=\"M296 140 L296 186\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#FF6A13\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"306\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">2 to 5 km</text><text x=\"104\" y=\"232\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">a year, one area</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"284\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Migrants: 20 to 25% (moderate snow zone)</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"304\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">70% or more (deep snow zone)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Some deer stay in one area all year; some move between summer and winter ranges <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>The deeper the snow, the more deer migrate <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: residents, migrants and home ranges</summary><ul><li>South coast study data, mostly Vancouver Island (90%, Nyberg and Janz 1990):<ul><li>Seasonal ranges of migrants are often 2 to 5 km apart.</li><li>Moderate snowpack zone: about 20 to 25% of deer migrate. Deep snowpack zone: 70% or more.</li><li>Summer home ranges of residents: about 55 ha.</li><li>Resident deer in the moderate zone: about 140 ha a year. Migrants there: about 1,770 ha a year.</li><li>Winter ranges: 48 ha in the deep zone, 36 ha in the shallow zone.</li></ul></li><li>WDFW (Washington Department of Fish and Wildlife): black tails normally live in about 1.3 to 7.8 km2 (half to 3 square miles); in the mountains they move lower for winter <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>What it means for you: a deer you see in October is probably close by in November, unless snow moves it (Tip).</li><li>Which zone is your spot in? Use the elevation bands on the next screen (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 325\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"The same snowfall left about 50 cm of snow in a clearcut and about 5 cm under a conifer stand with 70% crown closure. A black tailed deer chest is about 57 cm high. Walking cost rose about 500% in the clearcut and 10% in the forest.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"325\" fill=\"#EEF1EA\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Why winter deer hide under big trees</text>\n<rect x=\"14\" y=\"46\" width=\"180\" height=\"222\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"206\" y=\"46\" width=\"180\" height=\"222\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Clearcut</text><text x=\"296\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Conifer stand</text><rect x=\"34\" y=\"170\" width=\"140\" height=\"80\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#9aa8b8\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"220\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">about 50 cm</text><path d=\"M246 140 L270 84 L294 140 Z M300 140 L324 84 L348 140 Z\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"226\" y=\"242\" width=\"140\" height=\"8\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#9aa8b8\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"296\" y=\"232\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">about 5 cm</text><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"159\" x2=\"376\" y2=\"159\" stroke=\"#FF6A13\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 4\"/><rect x=\"104\" y=\"137\" width=\"192\" height=\"21\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#EEF1EA\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"152\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#FF6A13\">deer chest height 57 cm</text><text x=\"104\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">walking cost +500%</text><text x=\"296\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">walking cost +10%</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"312\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Same snowfall, 70% crown closure</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Same snowfall: about 50 cm in a clearcut, about 5 cm under 70% conifer cover <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Snow makes walking costly for deer; cover saves them energy <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: snow numbers</summary><ul><li>All from Nyberg and Janz 1990, south coast data <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>:<ul><li>An adult black tail's chest is about 57 cm high.</li><li>Once a deer sinks past about 40% of its chest height, energy use climbs fast. Sinking more than 25 cm restricts coast black tails a lot.</li><li>A doe sinking 30 cm spends about 6 times the energy of one sinking 10 cm. A fawn sinking 30 cm: 8 to 10 times.</li><li>A late winter 50 cm of snow in a fresh clearcut left about 5 cm under a conifer stand with 70% crown closure. Walking cost: about 500% more in the clearcut, about 10% more in the forest.</li><li>In cold, snowy spells deer used less than 40% of the area they used in mild winters.</li><li>Red huckleberry and salal grow tallest under moderately closed canopies. Salal is buried faster by snow than red huckleberry.</li></ul></li><li>What it means for you: after a heavy snow, look under big timber and on sunny slopes, not in open cuts (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 318\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Capable deer winter range in order U-2-006: slopes facing 135 to 285 degrees, south east through south to west, shown in orange on a compass. Slopes 40 to 100%, not shaded by nearby hills. Snow zones: under 400 m shallow, 400 to 800 m moderate, over 800 m deep.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"318\" fill=\"#EEF1EA\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Winter range: how to spot it</text>\n<rect x=\"14\" y=\"46\" width=\"186\" height=\"234\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"206\" y=\"46\" width=\"180\" height=\"234\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"107\" cy=\"160\" r=\"66\" fill=\"#c9d3c0\" stroke=\"none\"/><path d=\"M107 160 L153.7 206.7 A66 66 0 0 1 43.2 142.9 Z\" fill=\"#FF6A13\"/><text x=\"107\" y=\"85\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">N</text><text x=\"187\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">E</text><text x=\"107\" y=\"245\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">S</text><text x=\"27\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">W</text><text x=\"107\" y=\"266\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">Faces 135 to 285 degrees</text><rect x=\"220\" y=\"64\" width=\"152\" height=\"54\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"296\" y=\"86\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">over 800 m</text><text x=\"296\" y=\"106\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">deep snow</text><rect x=\"220\" y=\"134\" width=\"152\" height=\"54\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"296\" y=\"156\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">400 to 800 m</text><text x=\"296\" y=\"176\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">moderate</text><rect x=\"220\" y=\"204\" width=\"152\" height=\"54\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#e3ead9\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"296\" y=\"226\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">under 400 m</text><text x=\"296\" y=\"246\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">shallow</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"304\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Slopes 40 to 100%, in the sun (95%)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>An <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"UWR\">UWR</button> (Ungulate Winter Range) order covers deer winter range in the Chilliwack district: U-2-006 <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>.</li><li>Its polygons sit on both sides of Harrison Lake and in the Chehalis area (85%, my reading of the map layer).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: what the order says</summary><ul><li>Order U-2-006, made under the Government Actions Regulation, sets ungulate winter range for mule deer and Columbian black tailed deer in the Fraser TSA (Timber Supply Area), Chilliwack district (95%, read directly).</li><li>Approved 22 September 2009, amended May 2011; about 31,838 ha (95%, BC approved UWR list).</li><li>\"Capable winter range\": warm aspects 135 to 285 degrees (south east through south to west), slopes 40 to 100%, not shaded by nearby hillsides; gentle snow free slopes in the shallow zone count too <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>.</li><li>Snow zones in the order: shallow under 400 m, moderate 400 to 800 m, deep over 800 m <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>.</li><li>Hunt Mentor read the government shapefile: 115 polygons, all coded for mule deer (black tail and mule deer). Groups sit east of Harrison Lake, west of it toward the Chehalis, north of the lake and up the Fraser Canyon (85%, my reading of polygon positions).</li><li>A UWR limits logging and roads. It does not close hunting by itself (85%, my reading). Hunting rules: <a href=\"#/s/local-harrison-deer\">Your Harrison deer hunt</a>.</li><li>More: <a href=\"#/s/winter-range-legal\">Winter ranges and the law</a>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 330\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Black tailed deer food. Winter and early spring: Douglas fir, western redcedar, red huckleberry, salal, deer fern, tree lichens. Late spring to fall: grasses, trailing blackberry, fireweed, pearly everlasting, willow and maple leaves, salmonberry and salal.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"330\" fill=\"#EEF1EA\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">What they eat on the coast</text>\n<rect x=\"14\" y=\"46\" width=\"180\" height=\"250\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"206\" y=\"46\" width=\"180\" height=\"250\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Winter, early spring</text><text x=\"296\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Late spring to fall</text><circle cx=\"30\" cy=\"97\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"42\" y=\"102\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Douglas fir</text><circle cx=\"222\" cy=\"97\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"234\" y=\"102\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Grasses</text><circle cx=\"30\" cy=\"129\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"42\" y=\"134\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Western redcedar</text><circle cx=\"222\" cy=\"129\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"234\" y=\"134\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Trailing blackberry</text><circle cx=\"30\" cy=\"161\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"42\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Red huckleberry</text><circle cx=\"222\" cy=\"161\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"234\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Fireweed</text><circle cx=\"30\" cy=\"193\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"42\" y=\"198\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Salal</text><circle cx=\"222\" cy=\"193\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"234\" y=\"198\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Pearly everlasting</text><circle cx=\"30\" cy=\"225\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"42\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Deer fern</text><circle cx=\"222\" cy=\"225\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"234\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Willow, maple leaves</text><circle cx=\"30\" cy=\"257\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"42\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Tree lichens</text><circle cx=\"222\" cy=\"257\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"234\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">Salmonberry, salal</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"318\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Food changes: so do the deer (90%)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Winter: Douglas fir, redcedar, red huckleberry, salal, deer fern, tree lichens <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Late spring to fall: grasses, forbs, shrub leaves <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-salal.jpg\" alt=\"Salal: thick oval leaves. Winter food and cover.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Salal: thick oval leaves. Winter food and cover. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Robert Flogaus-Faust, CC BY 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gaultheria_shallon_RF.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: food and where to find it</summary><ul><li>Winter and early spring foods: Douglas fir, western redcedar, red huckleberry, salal, deer fern and lichens that grow on trees (90%, BC deer account).</li><li>Late spring to fall: grasses, trailing blackberry, fireweed, pearly everlasting and other forbs; leaves of willow, salmonberry, salal, maple and other shrubs and trees <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Deer pick food carefully by taste, smell and sight (90%, Nyberg and Janz).</li><li>Wind thrown trees and fresh slash drop lichens and twigs in winter (85%, my reading of \"broken branches and the lichens\" in the BC account).</li><li>Plant photos: <a href=\"#/s/browse-plants\">Browse plants</a>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Top view of a cutblock 5 to 15 years old, full of young shrubs, next to standing timber. The orange edge where the two meet is where deer feed in the open and slip back to cover. A newer burn nearby is marked as often closed to vehicles.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" opacity=\".8\"/>\n  <path d=\"M60 50 L300 40 L330 200 L80 230 Z\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/>\n  <g fill=\"#8a9a6b\"><circle cx=\"110\" cy=\"90\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"220\" cy=\"80\" r=\"10\"/><circle cx=\"260\" cy=\"150\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"180\" r=\"8\"/><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"190\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"100\" r=\"7\"/><circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"70\" r=\"7\"/></g>\n  <path d=\"M60 50 L300 40 L330 200 L80 230 Z\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"6\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(318 130)\"><ellipse rx=\"14\" ry=\"7\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><circle cx=\"-12\" cy=\"-8\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/></g>\n  <g transform=\"translate(90 140)\"><ellipse rx=\"14\" ry=\"7\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"-8\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/></g>\n  <text x=\"195\" y=\"120\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Cutblock</text>\n  <text x=\"195\" y=\"140\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">5 to 15 years old</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"268\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffd34d\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Hunt the orange edge (60%)</text>\n  <text x=\"12\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\">timber = cover</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">feed in the block, cover a few steps away</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Mark young cutblocks next to standing timber (Tip).</li></ul><ul><li>Mark steep south to west slopes in big timber below snow (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: how to use the Hunt Map for this</summary><ul><li>Open the <a href=\"#/map\">Hunt Map</a>. Turn on the cutblock and land layers. Look for young cuts with timber on at least one side (Tip).</li><li>Chapter 5 has why: coast deer use most of a cut within 100 m of cover (90%, Nyberg and Janz).</li><li>Check the slope direction with the map's contour lines: south to west facing, steep, in timber (Tip).</li><li>Check no shooting areas and closed roads: <a href=\"#/s/local-harrison-deer\">Your Harrison deer hunt</a>.</li><li>Every spot is a candidate. Scout it before the hunt (Tip).</li></ul></details><aside class=\"callout rule\" role=\"note\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law): \"Green food in the open, big trees close by, sun on the slope. Find all three and sit.\"</p></aside>","<aside class=\"callout mistake\" role=\"note\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Hunting the middle of a big clearcut after a snow.</li><li>Walking flat dark timber with no food in it.</li><li>Taking a polygon on a map as a promise of deer. It is a guide, not a count.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\" role=\"note\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>Scouting day: find one young cutblock edge and one steep sunny timbered slope within 1 km of the truck. Look for tracks and droppings on both.</p></aside>","<ul><li>BC deer account 2000 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>. Nyberg and Janz 1990, pages 33 to 53 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>. UWR order U-2-006 and the approved UWR list (95%; map reading 85%). WDFW <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. Checked 2026-10-23.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: every source and link</summary><ul><li>BC Ministry of Environment, Lands and Parks (2000), <a href=\"https://www.env.gov.bc.ca/wld/documents/muledeer.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">\"Mule and Black-tailed Deer in British Columbia\"</a>, read directly 2026-10-23 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Nyberg and Janz, editors (1990), <a href=\"https://www.for.gov.bc.ca/hfd/pubs/docs/srs/Srs05.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">\"Deer and Elk Habitats in Coastal Forests of Southern British Columbia\"</a>, pages 33 to 53, read directly from page images 2026-10-23 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>BC Ministry of Environment, <a href=\"https://www.env.gov.bc.ca/wld/documents/uwr/u-2-006_order_22Sep09.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Order, Ungulate Winter Range U-2-006</a>, 22 September 2009, read directly 2026-10-23 <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>; <a href=\"https://www.env.gov.bc.ca/wld/frpa/uwr/approved_uwr.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">approved UWR list</a> <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>; <a href=\"https://www.env.gov.bc.ca/esd/distdata/ecosystems/frpa/uwr/r2/tuwra_u-2-006.zip\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">U-2-006 shapefile</a>, polygon positions read by Hunt Mentor <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Washington Department of Fish and Wildlife, <a href=\"https://wdfw.wa.gov/species-habitats/living/species-facts/deer\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Living with wildlife: deer</a>, read 2026-10-23 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul></details>"]